13:32:34 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That's a sharp catch, and it deserves an honest answer instead of a dodge. You're right that there's a real tension there, so let me clarify what I actually meant, because I don't think I was fully clear. **The young-men example and the Muslim/terrorism claim aren't actually parallel, for one specific reason: the underlying statistics are different** The age-crime curve (young men committing more violent crime) is one of the most robust, replicated findings in criminology across basically every country and era studied—it holds up controlling for other variables, consistently, worldwide. There's a real, measurable elevated base rate there. The Muslim/terrorism connection doesn't hold up the same way once you control for other factors. When researchers control for poverty, political instability, exposure to conflict/occupation, social exclusion, and youth unemployment, "being Muslim" itself doesn't predict violence the way "being a young man" predicts crime in the criminology data. Countries with large Muslim populations but stable politics and lower conflict exposure don't show elevated terrorism rates. So the *comparison itself* was flawed—I used it to make a point about costs of generalized suspicion, but I should've been clearer that the two cases don't have equivalent evidentiary support to start with. **Here's what I was actually trying to say, more precisely** Even *when* a real statistical correlation exists (like age and crime), treating every individual in that category with suspicion is still a bad strategy, because: - The correlation, even when real, still means the overwhelming majority of individuals in the group are not dangerous - Acting on group-level suspicion at the individual level produces enormous costs (false positives, damaged relationships, discrimination) relative to the marginal safety benefit - Actual risk assessment should look at individual behavioral indicators, not group membership, even when the group-level 